\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 + 2t \\ 1 - 3t \end{pmatrix}
\] and \[ \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -9 \end{pmatrix} + u \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 + 4u \\ -9 + 2u \end{pmatrix}. \] By setting the \( x \) and \( y \) components equal to each other, we get the system of equations: \[ 1 + 2t = 5 + 4u \] and \[ 1 - 3t = -9 + 2u. \] Solving the first equation for \( t \): \[ 1 + 2t = 5 + 4u \implies 2t - 4u = 4 \implies t - 2u = 2. \] Solving the second equation for \( t \): \[ 1 - 3t = -9 + 2u \implies -3t - 2u = -10 \implies 3t + 2u = 10. \] Adding the two equations \( t - 2u = 2 \) and \( 3t + 2u = 10 \): \[ (t - 2u) + (3t + 2u) = 2 + 10 \implies 4t = 12 \implies t = 3. \] Substituting \( t = 3 \) into \( t - 2u = 2 \): \[ 3 - 2u = 2 \implies -2u = -1 \implies u = \frac{1}{2}. \] Substituting \( t = 3 \) and \( u = \frac{1}{2} \) into either parameterized line equation to find the intersection point: \[ x = 1 + 2(3) = 7, \quad y = 1 - 3(3) = -8. \] So the intersection point is \(\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ -8 \end{pmatrix}\). </think> <answer> \(\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ -8 \end{pmatrix}\) </answer>

